---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import SchemaFields from '../components/SchemaFields.astro';

// Field rows are generated from the schema, so this page can't drift from it.
const schemaRaw = fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(process.cwd(), '../../schemas/context-manifest.schema.json'), 'utf-8');
const schema = JSON.parse(schemaRaw);
// A manifest that exercises every field; verified against the schema above.
const exampleRaw = fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(process.cwd(), 'src/data/manifest-complete.json'), 'utf-8');
---

<Doc
   title="The manifest (leji.json) · Leji"
   description="The leji.json manifest: the machine entrypoint of a Leji context layer, declaring its context root, boot profile, category index files, and conformance claim."
>
   <h1>The manifest: <code>leji.json</code></h1>
   <p class="sp-lede">{schema.description}</p>

   <p>
      The manifest is the only fixed filename in Leji: the one file tooling can always expect at the repository root.
      Field rows are generated from{' '}
      <a href="/schemas/context-manifest/">the schema</a>, so nothing here drifts from the contract. It is a summary of
      top-level and selected nested fields, not the whole of it: for deeply nested shapes such as an actor's roles and
      commands, read the schema itself. Required fields carry the gold mark.
   </p>

   <h2>Fields</h2>
   <SchemaFields schema={schema} />

   <h2>A worked example</h2>
   <p>
      A federated, organization-wide context layer that mounts a sibling, using far more of the manifest than a real one
      usually needs. Most context layers declare only the subset they use: <code>leji init</code> scaffolds a handful of these
      fields, and a <code>core</code> manifest is much shorter. Two things about the shape are easy to misread: each category
      points at authored <strong>index files</strong>, not at content directories, and <code>agents</code> is a directory
      of profiles rather than a load order, so binding one never causes it to be read.
   </p>
   <div class="manifest-example">
      <Code code={exampleRaw.trimEnd()} lang="json" theme="night-owl" wrap />
   </div>

   <p>
      See <a href="/spec/machine-readable-surface/">the machine-readable surface</a> for how the manifest relates to the index,
      changelog, and profiles, and{' '}
      <a href="/spec/versioning/">versioning</a> for the <code>leji</code> spec-line key.
   </p>
</Doc>

<style>
   .manifest-example :global(.astro-code) {
      background: var(--blue-deep) !important;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
      font-size: var(--code-fz);
      line-height: 1.6;
   }
</style>
